WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.680
A knitter asked, can I use this
card for any stitch pattern?

2
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.440
What do you think?

3
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.640
Well, let's go back to the
function of a punch card.

4
00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:15.760
If you have an electronic machine,
the ideas here are the same.

5
00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:20.800
The black holes tell the machine
which needles to select.

6
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.680
What happens to the stitches on those
needles depends on your carriage setting.

7
00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.240
I happen to know that this stitch pattern

8
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:35.160
was designed for a G-carriage, also
called an automatic knitting carriage.

9
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:40.280
These were made for the Brother,
Knit King, standard gauge machines.

10
00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.040
The knitting will look like this.

11
00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:46.800
Can you see both knit and purl stitches?

12
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.840
But what if we tried to knit
this as a tuck stitch?

13
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.760
We can't knit tuck needles side by side.

14
00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:01.120
Remember how Tuck stitch is formed.
The yarn loops over the needles

15
00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:08.080
and needles right next to each other will
get great big loops and just make a mess.

16
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.080
So in viewing this card,

17
00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:17.080
we can immediately see that there are
holes side by side, not good for tuck.

18
00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.320
The same thing goes for slip.

19
00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:25.480
Slipping lots of needles side by side
will create really long floats. Again,

20
00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.720
remember how slip stitches formed.

21
00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.400
The working yarn is carried under
the needles, forming floats.

22
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:36.400
Short floats can be decorative.

23
00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:43.360
But this row is 24 stitches wide
and we'd have a 24 stitch float.

24
00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:48.400
Think of free pass where
there's no needles knitting.

25
00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.840
Could this stitch pattern
be used for Fair Isle?

26
00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:56.480
Well, I suppose, but every other row would

27
00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:02.000
be yellow in this case and the blue would
be carried across. Again,

28
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:07.120
really long floats and it's not
particularly attractive anyways.

29
00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:12.240
So the bottom line, can you use this
card for other stitch patterns?

30
00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:13.920
No.

31
00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:18.960
You can tell by examining the position
of the holes on the stitch pattern.

